  <command:command xmlns:maml="http://schemas.microsoft.com/maml/2004/10" xmlns:command="http://schemas.microsoft.com/maml/dev/command/2004/10" xmlns:dev="http://schemas.microsoft.com/maml/dev/2004/10" xmlns:MSHelp="http://msdn.microsoft.com/mshelp">
    <command:details>
      <command:name>Get-CertToolboxTlsCertificate</command:name>
      <command:verb>Get</command:verb>
      <command:noun>CertToolboxTlsCertificate</command:noun>
      <maml:description>
        <maml:para>Connects to a TLS endpoint and reports the negotiated session, the certificate chain the server sent, and the chain this machine builds from it.</maml:para>
      </maml:description>
    </command:details>
    <maml:description>
      <maml:para>One connection gives both views of the chain, and three properties comparing them.</maml:para>
      <maml:para>ServerChain holds the certificates the server actually sent, leaf first. This is what every client receives, whatever its own stores contain.</maml:para>
      <maml:para>LocalChain holds the chain this machine built to a root, using the server's certificates, its own certificate stores and, where the platform does it, downloaded intermediates. Each element carries its own Status flags (empty when it has no error).</maml:para>
      <maml:para>MissingIntermediate lists the certificates the local chain needed that the server did not send (roots excluded). Clients that cannot find them on their own will fail.</maml:para>
      <maml:para>ServerSentRoot is true when the server sent a self-signed certificate after the leaf, which is useless.</maml:para>
      <maml:para>UnusedServerCertificate lists the certificates the server sent that this machine did not use.</maml:para>
      <maml:para>The connection is never refused because of the certificate: validation problems are reported in ValidationPassed, PolicyError and ChainStatus instead, so that broken endpoints can be inspected.</maml:para>
      <maml:para>The handshake is done by the operating system's TLS stack. The protocol versions and cipher suites that can be offered therefore depend on the machine this runs on, and CipherSuite is only available under PowerShell 7 and later.</maml:para>
      <maml:para>Mail servers that start in plaintext and upgrade to TLS are reached with the StartTls parameter: the cmdlet speaks just enough SMTP, IMAP or POP3 to check that the server offers the upgrade and to request it, then does the same handshake. Use the Verbose switch to see that dialogue.</maml:para>
      <maml:para>With the TestCertificateUrl switch, every CRL, OCSP and issuer-certificate (CA Issuers) URL found in the certificates of both chains is requested, and the UrlTest property lists one result per certificate and URL: whether it answered, the dates it gave, and a Result. For a CRL the Result is NotRevoked, Revoked or CrlExpired; for OCSP it is Good, Revoked, Unknown or ResponderError; for CA Issuers it is IssuerMatch, IssuerMismatch or NotACertificate; and for any of them Failed (no usable answer) or Skipped (not testable, with the reason in Detail).</maml:para>
      <maml:para>These tests show whether the revocation and issuer infrastructure answers and what it says. They do not verify the signature of a CRL or of an OCSP response, so they are a diagnosis and not a trust decision; CheckRevocation gives the operating system's own verdict. They contact the certificate authorities' servers, over plain HTTP as those URLs require.</maml:para>
    </maml:description>
    <command:syntax>
      <command:syntaxItem>
        <maml:name>Get-CertToolboxTlsCertificate</maml:name>
        <command:parameter required="true" variableLength="true" globbing="false" pipelineInput="True (ByPropertyName, ByValue)" position="1" aliases="ComputerName, DnsName">
          <maml:name>HostName</maml:name>
          <maml:description>
            <maml:para>One or more DNS names or IP addresses to connect to.</maml:para>
          </maml:description>
          <command:parameterValue required="true" variableLength="false">String[]</command:parameterValue>
          <dev:type>
            <maml:name>String[]</maml:name>
            <maml:uri />
          </dev:type>
          <dev:defaultValue>None</dev:defaultValue>
        </command:parameter>
        <command:parameter required="false" variableLength="true" globbing="false" pipelineInput="True (ByPropertyName)" position="named" aliases="none">
          <maml:name>Port</maml:name>
          <maml:description>
            <maml:para>TCP port. Defaults to 443, or with StartTls to the protocol's standard port: 25 for Smtp, 143 for Imap, 110 for Pop3.</maml:para>
          </maml:description>
          <command:parameterValue required="true" variableLength="false">Int32</command:parameterValue>
          <dev:type>
            <maml:name>Int32</maml:name>
            <maml:uri />
          </dev:type>
          <dev:defaultValue>443</dev:defaultValue>
        </command:parameter>
        <command:parameter required="false" variableLength="true" globbing="false" pipelineInput="True (ByPropertyName)" position="named" aliases="ServerName, SNI">
          <maml:name>TargetHost</maml:name>
          <maml:description>
            <maml:para>Name sent as SNI and matched against the certificate, when it differs from HostName (for example when connecting to an IP address or to one node behind a load balancer).</maml:para>
          </maml:description>
          <command:parameterValue required="true" variableLength="false">String</command:parameterValue>
          <dev:type>
            <maml:name>String</maml:name>
            <maml:uri />
          </dev:type>
          <dev:defaultValue>None</dev:defaultValue>
        </command:parameter>
        <command:parameter required="false" variableLength="true" globbing="false" pipelineInput="False" position="named" aliases="none">
          <maml:name>Protocol</maml:name>
          <maml:description>
            <maml:para>Protocol versions to offer. SystemDefault (the default) lets the operating system choose. Use Test-CertToolboxTlsProtocol to probe each version separately.</maml:para>
          </maml:description>
          <command:parameterValue required="true" variableLength="false">String[]</command:parameterValue>
          <dev:type>
            <maml:name>String[]</maml:name>
            <maml:uri />
          </dev:type>
          <dev:defaultValue>@('SystemDefault')</dev:defaultValue>
        </command:parameter>
        <command:parameter required="false" variableLength="true" globbing="false" pipelineInput="False" position="named" aliases="none">
          <maml:name>TimeoutSec</maml:name>
          <maml:description>
            <maml:para>Timeout, in seconds, for the TCP connection and for each step of the handshake.</maml:para>
          </maml:description>
          <command:parameterValue required="true" variableLength="false">Int32</command:parameterValue>
          <dev:type>
            <maml:name>Int32</maml:name>
            <maml:uri />
          </dev:type>
          <dev:defaultValue>10</dev:defaultValue>
        </command:parameter>
        <command:parameter required="false" variableLength="true" globbing="false" pipelineInput="False" position="named" aliases="none">
          <maml:name>CheckRevocation</maml:name>
          <maml:description>
            <maml:para>Also checks certificate revocation while building the local chain.</maml:para>
          </maml:description>
          <dev:type>
            <maml:name>SwitchParameter</maml:name>
            <maml:uri />
          </dev:type>
          <dev:defaultValue>False</dev:defaultValue>
        </command:parameter>
        <command:parameter required="false" variableLength="true" globbing="false" pipelineInput="True (ByPropertyName)" position="named" aliases="none">
          <maml:name>StartTls</maml:name>
          <maml:description>
            <maml:para>The mail protocol to speak in plaintext before upgrading to TLS: Smtp, Imap or Pop3. Omit it for a server that expects TLS from the first byte (HTTPS, SMTPS on 465, IMAPS on 993, POP3S on 995).</maml:para>
          </maml:description>
          <command:parameterValue required="true" variableLength="false">String</command:parameterValue>
          <dev:type>
            <maml:name>String</maml:name>
            <maml:uri />
          </dev:type>
          <dev:defaultValue>None</dev:defaultValue>
        </command:parameter>
        <command:parameter required="false" variableLength="true" globbing="false" pipelineInput="False" position="named" aliases="none">
          <maml:name>SmtpEhloName</maml:name>
          <maml:description>
            <maml:para>The name announced in the SMTP EHLO command, with StartTls Smtp. Defaults to this machine's host name; set it when a server rejects or delays that name.</maml:para>
          </maml:description>
          <command:parameterValue required="true" variableLength="false">String</command:parameterValue>
          <dev:type>
            <maml:name>String</maml:name>
            <maml:uri />
          </dev:type>
          <dev:defaultValue>None</dev:defaultValue>
        </command:parameter>
        <command:parameter required="false" variableLength="true" globbing="false" pipelineInput="False" position="named" aliases="none">
          <maml:name>TestCertificateUrl</maml:name>
          <maml:description>
            <maml:para>Also requests every CRL, OCSP and CA Issuers URL found in the certificates of both chains, and reports the outcome in the UrlTest property. TimeoutSec applies to each request.</maml:para>
          </maml:description>
          <dev:type>
            <maml:name>SwitchParameter</maml:name>
            <maml:uri />
          </dev:type>
          <dev:defaultValue>False</dev:defaultValue>
        </command:parameter>
      </command:syntaxItem>
    </command:syntax>
    <command:parameters>
      <command:parameter required="true" variableLength="true" globbing="false" pipelineInput="True (ByPropertyName, ByValue)" position="1" aliases="ComputerName, DnsName">
